**The idea:** the essay is about a ruled form whose by-product outlived its purpose — so the page is built like the logbook it describes.

- **Typography.** Instrument Serif for display (high-contrast, engraved-feeling), Newsreader for the prose, IBM Plex Mono for everything administrative — kickers, labels, note numbers, read times. That mono/serif split is the design's whole argument: the record-keeping layer looks like record-keeping, the writing looks like writing.
- **Palette.** Warm paper (#e9e4d9) with a fixed hairline rule-grid behind everything, ink near-black, and one lamp amber used only where the light is the actual subject — the italic word *accidentally* in the headline, the drop cap, the section marks, the quote marks.
- **Marginal rail.** Each section gets a sticky left margin carrying `§ 01` and an italic slug ("The form produced the restraint", "1988 · sensor package installed") — the publication's name, made literal. It folds into a slug line above the heading under 820px.
- **Lede.** Replaced the grey placeholder with a drawn SVG plate: a Fresnel lens seen head-on, slowly rotating beams, and the sea ruled into visibility bands. The caption is set as an archive entry ("Plate I").
- **Reading structure.** Drop cap with small-caps first line opening the essay; a full-measure pull quote ruled top and bottom instead of the indented bar; notes as a two-column catalogue; related articles as ledger rows that indent on hover; the subscribe field as a line to write on rather than a box, over ruled paper.
- **Motion, quiet.** A 2px amber progress line, a section-tick rail at far left that tracks position via IntersectionObserver, a lamp pulse on the wordmark. All of it drops under `prefers-reduced-motion`.

Every preserved item is intact: full text and headings, headline/standfirst/byline, lede image and caption, pull quote, all three notes, all three related articles with read times, the subscribe form and its success state, and the four footer links.
